  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The atomic number of Neodymium (Nd) is 60. Its electronic configuration is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mi>Xe</mi></mfenced><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>6</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>.</p><p>Now, by removing two electrons from the outermost shell 6s,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Nd</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>&nbsp;ion is formed. Hence, electronic configuration of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Nd</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>&nbsp;is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mi>Xe</mi></mfenced><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></math>.</p><p>&nbsp;</p>"
